const LOCAL_POSTS = [
  {
    slug:"Field note", date:"2026", meta:"Field note · 2026 · 7 min",
    path:"/field-notes/ai-isnt-making-me-dumb",
    title:"AI isn't making me dumb. It's making me uncomfortable.",
    accent:"making me uncomfortable",
    byline:"Field Notes, 2026. About 7 min.",
    body:`<p>The worry everyone voices about AI tooling is that it makes you worse at your craft. That has not
been my experience. What it does is stranger and harder to talk about: it produces work faster than
I can form an opinion about it.</p>

<h2 data-toc>The cognitive quicksand</h2>
<p>There is a moment on every AI-assisted project where the output is good, the tests pass, and I
could not tell you why it is shaped the way it is. Not because it is obscure. Because nobody wrote
it. It arrived, it worked, and I moved on.</p>

<blockquote><p>Speed is not the problem. Speed without a reader is the problem.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>Take this with you</h2>
<p>Before accepting AI-written work, write one sentence explaining why it is shaped that way. If you
cannot, you have found the gap.</p>

<p><em class="todo">Opening of a published Field Notes post. Migrate the full text here and set the Substack
version to canonical back to this page.</em>
<a href="https://dsfieldnotes.substack.com/p/ai-isnt-making-me-dumb-its-making">Read on Substack →</a></p>`
  },
  {
    slug:"Field note", date:"May 2026", meta:"Field note · May 2026 · 6 min",
    path:"/field-notes/the-second-brain-problem",
    title:"The Second Brain Problem Nobody Talks About.",
    accent:"Nobody Talks About",
    byline:"Field Notes, May 2026. About 6 min.",
    body:`<p>I spent years building knowledge systems in Notion. They all died the same way, and it was never
the way the productivity people warn you about.</p>

<h2 data-toc>How they die</h2>
<p>Not from neglect. From success at the wrong thing. Capture gets easy, capture gets fast, and
synthesis — the slow part, the part that was the whole point — never scales to match. What you end
up maintaining is an archive of intentions.</p>

<blockquote><p>The system was never short of input. It was short of anyone thinking.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>Take this with you</h2>
<p>Measure a knowledge system by what you have written out of it, not by what you have put into it.</p>

<p><em class="todo">Opening of a published Field Notes post. Migrate the full text here.</em>
<a href="https://dsfieldnotes.substack.com/p/the-second-brain-problem-nobody-talks">Read on Substack →</a></p>`
  }
];

const POSTS = Array.isArray(window.__SUBSTACK_POSTS__) && window.__SUBSTACK_POSTS__.length
  ? window.__SUBSTACK_POSTS__
  : LOCAL_POSTS;

const WORK_ITEMS = [
  {
    slug:"Case study", meta:"Case study · Beroe · Platform architecture · 2024",
    path:"/work/search-as-home",
    home:"Search as home",
    title:"What happens when the homepage becomes the guide?",
    accent:"the homepage becomes the guide",
    byline:"Timeyin Gordon, Product Design at Beroe. 2024. About 10 min.",
    body:`
<p>Beroe's platform is a robust ecosystem for procurement professionals to explore risks, monitor
suppliers and act on real-time intelligence. But its homepage was trying to be a catalogue, an
activity feed, a notification centre and a wayfinder at the same time. <strong>It exposed the
platform's complexity before it helped people act.</strong></p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product Design</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>2024</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Homepage architecture, search, assistant placement, notifications</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2 data-toc>The problem</h2>
<h3 class="claim">One surface was failing people at opposite ends of the learning curve.</h3>
<p>New users met dense modules before they understood what the platform could do. Returning users
had repetitive, unclear paths back to suppliers, reports and work already in progress. The
homepage displayed everything, but guided nobody.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/search-as-home/previous-dashboard.webp" alt="The previous Beroe dashboard, crowded with product modules, news, activity and notifications" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The previous dashboard asked users to interpret the platform before choosing what to do.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The reframe</h2>
<h3 class="claim">What if the homepage acted like a concierge?</h3>
<p>Rather than treat it as a static dashboard, we used scenario-based design borrowed from travel
apps and maps and organised the experience around intent. Four questions became the foundation:</p>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>What would you like to do?</strong> Open-ended, goal-oriented exploration through search
or modules.</p>
<p><strong>Continue from where you stopped?</strong> Contextual access to recent activity and pages
already visited.</p>
<p><strong>What is happening today?</strong> Time-sensitive data, critical updates and relevant
procurement insight.</p>
<p><strong>What should I discover?</strong> Recommended products and modules based on role and usage.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-toc>The first direction</h2>
<h3 class="claim">We translated each question into a visible module.</h3>
<p>A persistent search bar let users begin anywhere. Recent activity restored context. Live updates
surfaced what had changed, while recommendations introduced relevant products without tutorial-led
onboarding. Abi sat alongside the journey as a companion that could guide and answer without
interrupting it.</p>
<figure class="fig portrait"><img src="/work/search-as-home/concierge-direction.webp" alt="The initial concierge-style homepage organised around suggested activity, recent work, live updates and recommendations" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The first direction made the four questions explicit in the page architecture.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The operational constraint</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Users typically receive about 5,000 alerts.</h3>
<p>Volume scales with alert frequency, supplier count and portfolio breadth. We moved notifications
into a drawer, grouped similar alerts through consolidation, added iconography so categories could
be identified at a glance, and gave system alerts their own treatment so critical information
stood out without flooding the homepage.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/search-as-home/notification-system.webp" alt="Notification drawer explorations showing grouped alert categories and consolidated updates" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Consolidation turned thousands of alerts into grouped, recognisable and actionable updates.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The pivot</h2>
<h3 class="claim">In testing, users kept reaching for one thing: search.</h3>
<p>The four questions were useful, but they did not need four equally prominent destinations.
Users preferred one entry point, so we reorganised the experience around that behaviour. Search
stopped being a utility in the header and became the homepage itself.</p>
<p>Abi moved inside the search experience, where it could interpret a query, recover from an empty
result and suggest a more useful next question. Recent history, real-time intelligence and
recommended actions answered the original four questions implicitly rather than as separate
modules.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/search-as-home/search-with-abi.webp" alt="Beroe search results with Abi suggesting related questions when a query has no direct result" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Abi appears at the moment search needs help, rather than as a separate destination.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The final experience</h2>
<h3 class="claim">One clear starting point, with context waiting underneath it.</h3>
<p>The final direction paired search with a compact layer of Quick Action Cards tailored to recent
activity and role. The cards could introduce features, resume familiar tasks or surface a timely
piece of intelligence without turning the homepage back into a wall of modules. Notifications and
Abi remained close at hand, but neither competed with the primary action.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/search-as-home/final-homepage.webp" alt="Final Beroe homepage with a prominent search field, contextual quick actions and an Abi conversation card" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The final direction: search leads; quick actions, notifications and Abi support the next decision.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>Handoff</h2>
<h3 class="claim">The prototype became part of the implementation conversation.</h3>
<p>As the work matured, we moved from Figma to Framer and then to Subframe, generating React
components integrated with Tailwind. Engineering received production-oriented components that
preserved the interactions and visual decisions, reducing the translation gap between design and
build.</p>

<blockquote><p>AI must live where decisions happen.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>What I learned</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Systems can be simple, but not simplistic.</h3>
<p>Compressing a multi-module homepage into one search-led experience only worked because we first
mapped the complexity underneath it. The concierge direction was not wasted work; it revealed the
intents that search eventually had to support.</p>
<h3 class="claim">AI needs integration, not decoration.</h3>
<p>Embedding Abi within the journey rather than tacking it on is what let it become useful instead
of intrusive.</p>
`
  },
