    title:"How do you turn complex risk data into a decision?",
    accent:"into a decision",
    byline:"Timeyin Gordon, Product Design at Beroe. 2024. About 9 min.",
    body:`
<p>Beroe's Supplier and Category Watch pages are mission-critical tools for businesses monitoring
risk across their supply chains. <strong>The interface was a barrier to the thing it existed to
support.</strong></p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product Design</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>2024</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Journey architecture, AI placement, data visualisation, layout system</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2 data-toc>The problem</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Users were handed risk data and left to interpret it alone.</h3>
<p>Navigation was disjointed across multiple tabs and filters. Data presentation was overwhelming
enough to obscure the insight inside it. There were no actionable recommendations, so every complex
risk scenario had to be interpreted manually. And Abi, the AI assistant, sat underused despite
being the obvious vehicle for exactly that guidance.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>Previous state — Supplier and Category Watch</span></div><figcaption>Before.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The decision ladder</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Four questions, answered in the order a buyer actually asks them.</h3>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>What is happening?</strong> Overview and general insight.</p>
<p><strong>Why is it happening?</strong> Root cause analysis.</p>
<p><strong>What has it done?</strong> Impact assessment.</p>
<p><strong>What can I do about it?</strong> Actionable recommendations.</p>
</div>
<p>Restructuring the journey around that sequence cut cognitive load, and removed the scrolling and
lateral navigation between disconnected parts of the interface.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>Journey architecture and information hierarchy</span></div><figcaption>Content ordered by decision, not by data type.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>Where the assistant belongs</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Abi moved from a peripheral tool to a central feature of the interface.</h3>
<p>Not because it needed more presence, but because the moments of uncertainty in this journey are
identifiable and recurring — and that is where an assistant earns its place.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Making the data speak</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Dynamic visualisation and narrative-driven insight turn complex data into something a user can retell.</h3>
<p>A buyer has to defend a decision internally. Data they can narrate is data they can act on.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Progressive disclosure</h2>
<p>We adopted a bento layout: an at-a-glance overview on landing, expandable detail drawers that go
deep without losing context, modular blocks that adapt to need, and a clear visual line between
summary and supporting data.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>Bento layout and detail drawer</span></div><figcaption>Overview first, depth on demand.</figcaption></figure>

<blockquote><p>Showing less initially can reveal more value.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>What I learned</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Information architecture is strategy, not housekeeping.</h3>
<p>The biggest improvement came from restructuring around decision-making patterns rather than
technical data organisation. Answering what, why and what next in sequence turned data consumption
into a workflow.</p>
<h3 class="claim">AI integration requires intentional design.</h3>
<p>Having the capability is not enough. Abi's success relied on appearing at moments of user
uncertainty with a specific, valuable recommendation.</p>
`
