    slug:"Case study", meta:"Case study · Beroe · Design systems · 2024",
    path:"/work/visualization-library",
    home:"Design systems",
    title:"Why we built the visualization library instead of adopting one.",
    accent:"instead of adopting one",
    byline:"Timeyin Gordon, Product Design at Beroe. 2024. About 11 min.",
    body:`
<p>Adopting a charting library is the obvious call. It is faster, it is cheaper, it is better
supported, and it is what I would recommend to most teams. <strong>We did not do it, and the reason
is the whole case study.</strong></p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product Design</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>2024</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>45+ chart types on Visx, token system, Figma library</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2 data-toc>Outcome</h2>
<h3 class="claim">45+ chart types on Visx, with one token set shared between the Figma library and the coded components.</h3>
<p>Adopted as the product default across surfaces. The rest of this is why that was worth doing
instead of installing something.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>The library — a grid of the chart types</span></div><figcaption>What shipped.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>What the product needed</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Charts here are not decoration on a report. They are the surface a buyer reads to decide whether to act.</h3>
<p>A procurement buyer is not browsing a dashboard for interest. They are deciding whether a supplier
risk is worth escalating, and then defending that decision to someone else. The chart is the
evidence in that argument.</p>
<p>Which means the series under discussion has to hold full visual weight while everything else
recedes — and crucially, <strong>that emphasis shifts as the conversation shifts.</strong> The same
chart has to argue different things at different moments.</p>

<h2 data-toc>The decision</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Emphasis lives in the interaction model, not the styling layer.</h3>
<p>This is the whole argument. A themed charting library gives you control over how a chart looks:
colour, type, spacing, density. It does not give you control over what the chart is asserting at a
given moment, because assertion is not a style property. It is a function of state — what the user
has selected, what the assistant has surfaced, what the page is currently about.</p>
<p>Building on Visx meant we owned the render layer and could bind emphasis to that state rather
than to a theme.</p>

<h2 data-toc>What we tried</h2>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">Exploration 1 — An off-the-shelf library with theming.</p>
  <p>Install, theme to brand, ship. Weeks rather than months, and a maintenance burden somebody
  else carries.</p>
  <p class="verdict">The right call in a product where charts report. Wrong in one where they argue
  — theming reaches the surface, not the behaviour underneath it.</p>
</div>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">Exploration 2 — Wrap a library and override the interaction layer.</p>
  <p>Keep the rendering, replace the parts that decide what is emphasised.</p>
  <p class="verdict"><em class="todo">Did you consider this? If so, why it failed — fighting the
  library's own state model, or the override surface being too small. If not, say so plainly; a
  case study that only lists one rejected option looks thin.</em></p>
</div>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">Solution — Build on Visx and own the emphasis model.</p>
  <p>Primitives rather than components, so the interaction model is ours and the visual language is
  a token set rather than a theme file.</p>
  <p class="verdict chosen">One system, two consumers: the Figma library and the coded components
  read the same tokens, so the file and the product cannot drift.</p>
</div>

<blockquote><p>Theming reaches the surface. Emphasis lives underneath it.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>Tokens, components, adoption</h2>
<p>This is the section that matters most to anyone hiring a design engineer, and the one only you
can write. The questions it needs to answer:</p>
<p><em class="todo">What is in the token layer — colour scales, emphasis states, density, motion?
How does a designer pick a chart in Figma, and what do they get in code when they do? What does
Storybook cover, and is there visual regression? Which team adopted first, how long did it take,
and what did you have to change to get the second team to take it? What drift existed before, and
what does zero drift mean in practice here?</em></p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>Figma library beside the shipped component</span></div><figcaption>The same tokens, both directions.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="fig"><div class="box"><span>Token structure and emphasis states</span></div><figcaption>How a chart knows what it is arguing.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>Evidence</h2>
<p>Adopted as the product default across surfaces at Beroe. Commercial outcomes are confidential;
everything above is stated at the level I can substantiate without them.</p>
<p><em class="todo">Add the component count, the number of teams using it, and the maintenance or
build-time difference if you have it.</em></p>

<h2 data-toc>What I learned</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Build versus adopt is a question about behaviour, not about looks.</h3>
<p>Every argument for adopting is about the surface — speed, cost, support, consistency. Every
argument for building was about what the thing had to do when a user interacted with it. If the
answer to "what does this need to do that a theme cannot express" is nothing, adopt.</p>
<h3 class="claim">A design system with two consumers is one system. With two sources of truth it is two systems that will disagree.</h3>
<p>Sharing the token layer between the Figma library and the components was the decision that made
the rest maintainable.</p>
`
  },
  {
    slug:"Venture", meta:"Venture · Neuro 6 · Live on Kickstarter",
    path:"/work/your-village-people",
