    meta:"Dustin Home · E-commerce · UX research",
    byline:"UX Researcher. Hyper Island MA Digital Management, in partnership with Dustin. 2024.",
    body:`
<p>Dustin is a leading B2B electronics retailer across the Nordics and Benelux. Its consumer
platform, Dustin Home, faced a different reality: two separate platforms split traffic, weakened
brand continuity and missed opportunities between customers who shop both professionally and
personally.</p>
<p>This applied academic project was a partnership between Hyper Island's MA Digital Management
programme and Dustin. My role was to turn observed checkout friction into evidence-backed
recommendations—not to claim an implementation or conversion outcome that the project did not
measure.</p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>UX Researcher</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Timeline</dt><dd>2024</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Hyper Island MA Digital Management × Dustin</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Research</dt><dd>5 interviews · 4 competitors · 6 usability criteria</dd></div>
</dl>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/dustin-home/project-overview.webp" alt="A collage of Dustin Home product, cart and checkout interfaces alongside the research team" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The project followed the consumer journey from product discovery to checkout.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>The challenge</h2>
<h3 class="claim">68.8% cart abandonment, while Dustin Home met only two of six benchmarked checkout criteria.</h3>
<p>The problem was broader than the final payment screen. Customers formed trust while searching,
comparing products, reading specifications and judging whether the retailer understood their
needs. Friction accumulated throughout the journey and arrived at checkout as hesitation.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Mapping the journey</h2>
<h3 class="claim">We looked for the moments where attention became confidence—or disappeared.</h3>
<p>Collaborative usability testing mapped friction from the homepage through checkout. The AIDA
framework—attention, interest, desire and action—helped identify the moments where customers chose
to continue, compare elsewhere or stop.</p>
<figure class="fig portrait"><img src="/work/dustin-home/aida-model.webp" alt="AIDA funnel showing awareness, interest, desire and action" loading="lazy"><figcaption>AIDA provided the scaffold for mapping decisions across the purchase journey.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/dustin-home/journey-map.webp" alt="Customer journey map documenting discovery, active evaluation and retailer choice during the desire stage" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The audit connected interface friction to the decisions customers were trying to make.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>Primary research</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Five Swedish participants made the same underlying need visible: confidence before commitment.</h3>
<p>We ran semi-structured, think-aloud interviews with five tech-savvy professionals in their late
twenties to mid-thirties. Individual empathy maps using the D.School method were synthesised to
identify recurring concerns: value for money, limited emotional connection, checkout complexity
and insufficient product reviews.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/dustin-home/empathy-map.webp" alt="Empathy map synthesising one participant's goals, behaviours, pains and trust needs" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Individual maps preserved participant context before findings were synthesised across the group.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>What we learned</h2>
<h3 class="claim">The experience was described as clean, but not always clear.</h3>
<p>Three of five participants preferred guest checkout. Four of five said they would not purchase
a product without adequate reviews. The current guest option existed, but lacked the placement and
visual hierarchy participants expected; account creation was presented before its value had been
earned.</p>

<blockquote><p>If there are no or few reviews, I will navigate away and search on Google.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>Competitive evidence</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Competitors made the guest path easier to recognise and delayed the account decision.</h3>
<p>We benchmarked Dustin Home and Dustin B2B against Komplett, Webhallen, Elgiganten and Inet across
six checkout criteria. Dustin Home met two. Webhallen, Elgiganten and Inet integrated guest
checkout into the flow, gave it greater visual priority and postponed account creation.</p>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/dustin-home/checkout-benchmark.webp" alt="Checkout benchmark comparing Dustin Home and Dustin B2B with Komplett, Webhallen, Elgiganten and Inet" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Six criteria exposed where Dustin's account and guest-checkout hierarchy differed from competitors.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 data-toc>Three recommendations</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Each recommendation traces directly to a research finding.</h3>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">1 — Build emotional clarity on top of utility.</p>
  <p>Participants trusted the functional offer but described little connection to the brand. Aaron
  Walter's emotional-design framework helped distinguish the reliable and usable foundation from
  the pleasurable layer the consumer experience was missing.</p>
  <p class="verdict chosen">Recommendation: strengthen personality, hierarchy, messaging and
  lifestyle-led product presentation without compromising the functional foundation.</p>
</div>
<figure class="fig portrait"><img src="/work/dustin-home/emotional-design-model.webp" alt="Emotional design model showing a pleasurable layer above functional, reliable and usable foundations" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The opportunity was not decoration; it was the missing pleasurable layer above an already functional base.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="fig"><img src="/work/dustin-home/homepage-direction.webp" alt="A Dustin Home interface direction with clearer navigation, promotional hierarchy and trust signals" loading="lazy"><figcaption>An interface direction exploring clearer hierarchy, personality and retail confidence.</figcaption></figure>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">2 — Delay the account decision.</p>
  <p>Guest checkout was technically available, but its placement and hierarchy made account creation
  feel like the expected route. Research showed that customers wanted to complete the purchase
  before deciding whether an account was worthwhile.</p>
  <p class="verdict chosen">Recommendation: make guest checkout the visual priority, explain the
  option plainly, then present account benefits on the confirmation page and in follow-up email.</p>
</div>

<div class="trial">
  <p class="try">3 — Put reviews where trust is formed.</p>
  <p>Four of five participants would leave the site when product reviews were absent or too few.
  Reviews were not an accessory to the product page; they were part of active evaluation.</p>
  <p class="verdict chosen">Recommendation: begin with Trustpilot integration for immediate
  coverage, share review evidence across purchase touchpoints, and develop an in-house capability
  for long-term experience control.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-toc>What the project produced</h2>
<h3 class="claim">A research-backed roadmap, not a fabricated conversion claim.</h3>
<p>The deliverable connected observed behaviour, journey friction, competitive evidence and
secondary research to three implementation directions. Whether those recommendations changed
conversion would require implementation and a measured post-launch comparison; this project did
not include that phase.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Reflection</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Checkout is where a retailer finds out what its information architecture actually costs.</h3>
<p>Every ambiguity upstream arrives at the payment step as hesitation. The most useful outcome of
the research was seeing checkout not as an isolated funnel screen, but as the final expression of
trust built—or lost—throughout the shopping journey.</p>
`
