const CRAFT_ITEMS = [
  {
    slug:"Side project", meta:"Ply · Canvas editor",
    path:"/craft/ply",
    marker:"01",
    title:"Ply — learning how a design canvas really works.",
    home:"Canvas editor",
    accent:"how a design canvas really works",
    byline:"An independent exploration into rendering, coordinate systems, nodes and overlays.",
    body:`
<p>Ply began as one of those curiosity projects that would not leave me alone. I wanted to know
what it takes to make a 2D canvas feel natural—not only how to draw one.</p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Product Designer + Frontend Engineer</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Team</dt><dd>Independent project</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>React, TypeScript, PixiJS, Zustand, Lexical, Motion, CSS Modules, Claude and Antigravity</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2 data-toc>The idea</h2>
<h3 class="claim">What if designing a product and defining how it works happened on the same canvas?</h3>
<p>I kept thinking about two tools that feel powerful for different reasons. Figma provides a
freeform surface where ideas can be moved around spatially. n8n provides a visual way to connect
triggers, actions and decisions into something that actually runs.</p>
<p>Today, those activities usually live in separate tools. An interface is designed in one place;
its behaviour is described in another. The connection is handed off through files, tickets,
meetings and interpretation.</p>
<p>My original vision for Ply was a shared environment where interface, logic and automation could
live together. A button would not only have a visual style; it could reveal the workflow behind it.
Designing and building would become different views of the same system rather than separate phases
on separate platforms.</p>
<p>I knew that was too much to build at once. Before design nodes and automation nodes could share a
canvas, I needed to understand the foundation beneath both:</p>
<blockquote><p>How do you build a fast, extensible 2D canvas?</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>Choosing an engine</h2>
<h3 class="claim">I expected a library decision. It became an architecture decision.</h3>
<p>One of the first projects I studied was <a href="https://github.com/toeverything/AFFiNE">AFFiNE</a>
and its open-source framework, <a href="https://github.com/toeverything/blocksuite">BlockSuite</a>.
BlockSuite grew out of AFFiNE's attempt to combine document editing with an edgeless whiteboard.
That was close to what I imagined, and it proved that documents and spatial canvases did not have
to remain separate.</p>
<p>But its assumptions were connected to its origins as a block-based knowledge tool. Ply needed
room for design objects, documents, executable workflow nodes, connectors, nested canvases and
interaction models I had not invented yet.</p>
<p>Starting with BlockSuite might have helped me move faster. It might also have meant inheriting
another product's idea of what a node should be.</p>
<blockquote><p>The fastest way to begin was not necessarily the best way to explore.</p></blockquote>

<h2 data-toc>The architecture bet</h2>
<h3 class="claim">“Canvas” can mean several very different things.</h3>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>The DOM.</strong> Native text, events and accessibility, but a large scene can repeatedly
trigger layout and paint work.</p>
<p><strong>Canvas 2D.</strong> Direct control over drawing, but the editor must remember the scene,
redraw it, order it and determine what the user clicked.</p>
<p><strong>A GPU-rendered scene.</strong> Well suited to transforming many visual objects, while
native text editing and interface controls remain stronger in the browser.</p>
</div>
<p>At first this looked like a CPU-versus-GPU decision. In reality, the CPU still has to understand
pointer input, update the document, calculate geometry and decide what changed. The GPU can then
draw and composite the result.</p>
<h3 class="claim">The document would not know which renderer drew it.</h3>
<p>Figma's description of its canvas as something closer to a game engine than a conventional web
page helped clarify the separation. Its custom canvas and surrounding React interface are distinct
systems. <a href="https://www.figma.com/blog/how-figma-draws-inspiration-from-the-gaming-world/">Figma's engineering write-up</a>
made me ask which parts of Ply should know how pixels are produced.</p>
<p>I made the editor core renderer-agnostic. Think of the document as a blueprint and the renderer
as a projector: the blueprint describes objects, positions, styles and relationships; the
projector decides how to display them. Replacing the projector should not invalidate the blueprint.</p>
<pre aria-label="Ply architecture"><code>React interface and DOM editors
              ↓
Document, selection, viewport and tools
              ↓
Renderer-independent editor core
              ↓
PixiJS renderer</code></pre>
<p>Tools operate on nodes, selections, transforms and commands rather than PixiJS objects. A small
renderer adapter translates those concepts into a Pixi scene. That cost more at the beginning but
protected the larger exploration: the renderer can be optimised or replaced without rebuilding
undo, tools or the document model.</p>

<h2 data-toc>The hybrid canvas</h2>
<h3 class="claim">The recurring decision was not which technology to choose, but where each should stop.</h3>
<p>PixiJS provides a GPU-backed renderer with containers, graphics, text, transforms and display
order without forcing Ply to work directly with WebGL or WebGPU. But the whole application does
not live inside PixiJS.</p>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>The GPU draws.</strong> PixiJS renders the grid, shapes, frames, text, paths, connectors
and selection graphics.</p>
<p><strong>The CPU decides.</strong> TypeScript manages the document, geometry, hit testing,
commands, tools and hierarchy.</p>
<p><strong>The DOM handles native interaction.</strong> React renders the toolbar, layers,
inspector, menus and text editors.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-toc>Making it feel native</h2>
<h3 class="claim">An infinite canvas can be mathematically correct and still feel wrong.</h3>
<p>Every object exists in at least two places. <strong>World space</strong> is its location on the
infinite document. <strong>Screen space</strong> is where the camera currently makes it appear.
Panning moves the camera; zooming changes its scale. The object itself has not moved.</p>
<p>Every pointer event must be translated through that camera before a tool can use it. A rectangle
tool should receive the same world position whether the canvas is at 25% or 400%.</p>
<pre><code>screen = (world + pan) × zoom
world = screen ÷ zoom - pan</code></pre>
<p>When someone zooms at the cursor, the point underneath it should remain in place. If it drifts,
the canvas feels as though it is escaping. Keeping that point stationary made the interaction feel
dramatically more natural.</p>

<h3 class="claim">Selection controls belong to the screen, not the world.</h3>
<p>Rendering resize handles with the objects they control makes them tiny when zoomed out and
enormous when zoomed in. I split the Pixi stage into three layers:</p>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>A grid</strong> covering the viewport.</p>
<p><strong>A world</strong> containing the actual nodes.</p>
<p><strong>A screen-space layer</strong> containing selections, hover outlines and handles.</p>
</div>
<p>Nodes move and scale with the camera. Controls follow their positions while keeping a consistent
physical size. It is nearly invisible when it works; when it does not, the editor immediately
feels like a prototype.</p>

<h3 class="claim">Nodes need to know where they live.</h3>
<p>Ply stores nodes in a flat collection for fast lookup. Parent and child IDs describe hierarchy.
A shared structure—identity, type, transform, style and properties—supports rectangles, documents,
sticky notes, connectors and workflow actions.</p>
<p>Top-level nodes use world positions; children use positions relative to their parent. Think of a
chair inside a room: if the room moves, the chair moves with it, while its position inside the room
does not change. Updating both the frame and its children would apply the translation twice.</p>
<p>The first transform system supports position, size and rotation. Full inherited rotation, scale
and skew will eventually require a matrix system. Starting smaller made the hierarchy rules visible
before making the maths more powerful.</p>

<h3 class="claim">Text reminded me that the browser is still incredibly useful.</h3>
<p>Displaying text and editing text are different problems. PixiJS can draw text, but editing needs
a caret, selection, clipboard behaviour, keyboard shortcuts, multiline input and eventually
accessibility.</p>
<p>When a user edits text, Ply places a real DOM editor over the canvas object and temporarily hides
the Pixi representation. Committing the edit saves content back to the document and restores GPU
rendering. Rich documents use Lexical; sticky notes and contextual controls use the same DOM-overlay
principle.</p>
<p>This hybrid approach felt less ideologically pure than “everything on canvas,” but it produced a
much better interaction model.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Tools and commands</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Pointer movement had to become understandable actions.</h3>
<p>The select tool is where the systems collide. At different moments, the same pointer may be
hovering, drawing a marquee, moving an object, resizing it or rotating it. Treating those as
unrelated event handlers quickly becomes confusing.</p>
<p>I modelled tools as small state machines. A tool always knows which interaction it is performing
and which transitions are allowed next.</p>
<p>Tools do not mutate the document directly. They emit commands such as “add node,” “update
transform” or “move selection.” Commands can be undone, redone, merged and grouped into
transactions. A drag becomes one understandable history entry—<strong>Move</strong>—instead of
hundreds of pointer updates.</p>
<p>That structure also supports bound connectors. Moving a node updates its connected edges as part
of the same interaction rather than as an unrelated rendering trick.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Performance</h2>
<h3 class="claim">The performance trick was to create two paths.</h3>
<p>The document should be the source of truth, but pushing every pointer movement through global
state and React puts work on the hottest interaction path.</p>
<div class="points">
<p><strong>Preview immediately.</strong> While the pointer moves, Ply updates the relevant Pixi
objects directly.</p>
<p><strong>Commit deliberately.</strong> On pointer release, one final command records the transform
in the document and history.</p>
</div>
<p>The preview path is optimised for responsiveness; the commit path is optimised for correctness.
Pixi's continuous ticker also stops while nothing changes. Render requests made during the same
browser frame are collected into one <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> callback, while the React
interface subscribes only to the state each control needs.</p>

<h3 class="claim">Measure without pretending the work is finished.</h3>
<p>A small panel shows FPS, average frame interval and node count. It reveals obvious degradation
and updates only four times per second so it does not create unnecessary React work.</p>
<p>But the monitor measures browser animation-frame rhythm, not isolated GPU render time, React
work, hit testing or pointer-to-pixel latency. It is instrumentation, not a benchmark. I avoided
turning “I saw 60 FPS” into a claim that the editor scales.</p>
<p>A proper test would create repeatable scenes containing 100, 1,000, 5,000 and 10,000 nodes and
measure frame time, input latency, renderer synchronisation, hit-test time, memory use and idle
activity.</p>

<h2 data-toc>Why CSS Modules</h2>
<h3 class="claim">I wanted the visual rules of each editor surface to be inspectable.</h3>
<p>The interface uses CSS Modules and shared custom properties rather than Tailwind. This is not
because utility classes are inherently slow. Ply contains floating panels, portal-based popovers,
draggable inputs, layered overlays and controls with precise interaction states.</p>
<p>Component-scoped CSS gives each surface a clear boundary. Shared variables still provide a small
design system for spacing, typography, colour, radii, layers and light and dark themes. React stays
focused on behaviour: the component decides what state it is in; the stylesheet describes what
that state looks like.</p>

<h2 data-toc>What remains</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Ply has foundations—and clear scaling limits.</h3>
<p>The editor now supports pan and zoom, shapes, frames, text, documents, hierarchy, grouping,
selection, transforms, undo, connectors, overlays and multiple canvas types.</p>
<p>The renderer accepts incremental document changes, but the current React bridge still traverses
every active node when the document changes. Hit testing checks nodes from front to back one at a
time. A quadtree exists for faster spatial searches but is not connected to the live renderer.</p>
<div class="points">
<p>Connect document diffs so the renderer processes only changed nodes.</p>
<p>Use the spatial index to narrow hit tests.</p>
<p>Replace simple parent offsets with full transform matrices.</p>
<p>Make every DOM overlay use one shared coordinate pipeline.</p>
<p>Build reproducible stress tests instead of relying on an FPS counter.</p>
</div>
<p>A few early overlays repeated the pan-and-zoom formula instead of using the shared conversion
function. Even one sign difference can make an editor drift away from its node. Coordinate maths
is not view decoration—it is part of the product's behaviour.</p>

<h2 data-toc>What I learned</h2>
<h3 class="claim">Drawing is only one part of making a canvas feel right.</h3>
<p>The camera has to agree with the pointer. Children have to agree with their parents. Selection
controls have to agree with both the world and the screen. Fast previews eventually have to agree
with durable state. The renderer matters, but the boundaries around it matter more.</p>
<p>Claude and Antigravity made it possible to explore a much wider technical surface quickly. They
could help generate a renderer, tool or node type. But speed made architectural discipline more
important, not less. I still had to decide which system owned the truth, inspect generated
assumptions and test whether an interaction actually felt right.</p>
<p>Ply has not yet reached the original vision of design and automation sharing one living canvas.
It has given me the foundation—and the mental model—to keep exploring it.</p>
<blockquote><p>The GPU draws. The CPU decides. The DOM handles the interactions it already does exceptionally well.</p></blockquote>
`
